ing  hunger strike that Mike alluded to the  refugees were on hunger strike when  Jesse Jackson went to Guantanamo I mean  he didn't just go right he Jackson up  until this point had never publicly  identified himself with any AIDS or HIV  related issue and in fact he was talking  about the plight of the Haitians who  were accused of being hiv-positive  ultimately he he went to went on among  he embraced folks there he said you  don't you know you can start eating now  I am going to go on hunger strike I'm  going to join your hunger strike and it  then became our job to mobilize others  to take that up and so Yale Law Students  as Mike said were in camped in the main  hallway in a makeshift cage with barbed  wire refusing food for a week they then  passed on that hunger strike to Harvard  Law School drove up to Harvard with all  the the pieces of the makeshift camp  that was taken over by Harvard and  ultimately I think over 50 different  schools I had student groups that took  over the hunger strike around the  country so that it could be sustained  over a period of months we would have  conference calls nationally where  student leaders from each of those  schools who were about to strike or had  had not yet done it yet said you know  what else can we do and the answer was  get your parents to write to the White  House and say that their kid is not  eating because you won't let those  refugees at camp and they did that  there was just a massive massive  grassroots mobilization that at the  beginning looked impossible because of  the contradictions embodied by our  client situation we ultimately were able  to make those contradictions work for us  and I think it's important to realize  that there is always opportunity in what  looks like adversity I won't belabor the  point but I want to emphasize that in  many important ways we ended up taking  our cues from our clients in the summer  of 92 there was a kind of a revolt in  the camps that's detailed in the books  which we learned about and pitch to the  Washington Post which wrote and one of  the first important mainstream stories  about it that was our clients activism  that prompted the post story by Lynn  Duke on my second trip to Vaughn tata  motors december of 92 so still predating  the hunger strikes the refugees wrote a  letter to many members of Congress  modeled on Martin Luther King's letter  from a Birmingham jail in which they  described their conditions very  eloquently and invited congressmen and  senators and so forth to come visit them  in their jail and we then carried it out  and delivered it and actually Claiborne  Pell who was then chair of the Senate  Foreign Relations Committee I think  partially in response that letter made  it quiet trip down there in the next  spring then they started the hunger  strike in February and that's really  what launched a lot up here but in many  ways it was their revolt their letter  their hunger strike that prompted our  advocacy here Casey I believe that  there's still an HIV exclusion rule at  least with regards to the issuance of  travel visas and I was wondering what  the current status of HIV exclusion is  with the asylum process and other  aspects of immigration policy you're  exposing my limitations as a so called  immigration expert it's still on the  books it was not repealed in any way but  it's always been right it's but it's  always been the case that there's been  discretionary ways around it and that's  what happened in our case and though it  remains a sort of important target  of immigrant rights advocates it's not  one that's sort of seen as achievable  through litigation right now bush line  let's let judge Johnson and Brandt then  have the last word Charlie it was an  interesting first big case for me as I  said before there are many issues in the  case that I learned about had never had  a TR 0 before I dealt with the  constitutional issues I was fortunate  enough to have a great law clerk twanna  Davis we discussed these saying we  walked late at night if that's any  consolation to you I was amazed to find  out how powerful some bureaucrats can be  and and the impact they have on the  lives of people we don't even know about  I recall that some of the people in  Guantanamo were so sick that the  American doctor said that they should  come to the United States to be treated  but bureaucrats in the Immigration  Department said no they could not come  that is powerful and nobody knew  anything about that and I said as long  as I have the case of bureaucrats not  gonna tell me what to do I'll tell them  what to do that I don't get headaches  out your headaches  I guess I want to make two points the  first is you've probably got in the  sense that this is an incredibly complex  set of cases there were two cases at and  and a number of Appeals my goal in  writing this book was not write a long  policy study or an in-depth doctrinal  analysis it was to write a book that  would be accessible to everybody it is  driven by plot and character you'll get  to know a lot about professor Co a lot  about Mike wish me about judge Johnson  about Lisa du Gard I basically set out  with the following a goal in mind I  wanted everybody in this law school to  find a book like this interesting it  didn't matter whether it was a law  student a law professor a secretary or  somebody who worked in construction  connected to the law school they should  all want to read the book they should  all care about and be excited by it or I  didn't do my job the second thing has to  do with what happened to the 300 people  on Guantanamo and I have two points to  make about that the first one is about  HIV exclusion we've had this right  federal regulation on the books for a  long time but we rarely enforce it  millions of people enter this country  legally every year we don't test them  for HIV and have even one half of one  percent of them are hiv-positive then  tens of thousands of people come in here  legally every year who have this  condition but the federal government  chose to focus just on this small group  of people these 300 people who are  democracy activists fleeing from a  brutal dictator in our own backyard why  apply it just to them why apply it just  to them well it certainly can't be  because of medical costs first as my  coition he told you we settled these  people without cost to the government I  should say that when i say we i mean the  folks who were involved in the case  second this this country allows in  people with heart conditions or kidney  conditions that cost every bit as much  as treating someone with a  in in the later stages of the of that of  that condition and and and I think that  um maybe the better way to look at the  group on Guantanamo is not to say ah you  know what bad things are going to happen  when they come in here but what  contributions might they make because  this is a country of refugees and  immigrants and everyone who comes here  ends up making a contribution and the  people who made it in thanks to the  order by Judge sterling Johnson well  today some of the kids there who are old  enough are now in college and planning  on careers there are taxicab drivers  there are construction workers there are  caterers there are musicians and let me  tell you last and most importantly about  Yvonne Pascal she is alive today and  doing quite well she works a full-time  job she pays for her own health care and  her older son is serving in the United  States Marines and just came back from  Iraq and that's the way I want to leave  it thank you
 

